<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hudson River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hudson River flows about 500 kilometres south from the Adirondack Mountains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> to the Atlantic at New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a> City. Named after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-hudson">Henry Hudson</a>, who sailed up it for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> in 1609, its valley was the core of New Netherland and the route between New York and Albany. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erie-canal">Erie Canal</a> linked it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-lakes">Great Lakes</a> in 1825, it became one of the chief trade arteries of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hudson-river.jpg" alt="Hudson River" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hudson_river_from_bear_mountain_bridge.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
